<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hotaru no Hikari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a> (Meiji 14)</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as 蛍の光</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Hotaru no Hikari&quot; (&quot;Glow of a firefly&quot;) is a Japanese song that sets new and entirely different words by Chikai Inagaki to the tune of the Scottish folk song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a>&quot;. It was first published in 1881 (Meiji 14) in a collection of songs for elementary school pupils. Putting new words to an existing tune without changing the music much is known as contrafactum.</p></div>
